> Jonathan Jones wrote:
>
>> On Fri, May 31, 2013 at 3:35 AM, Robert LeChevalier <lojbab@lojban.org
>> <mailto:lojbab@lojban.org>> wrote:
>>
>>     <snip>
>>     Personally, the thing that probably keeps most people from learning
>>     Lojban is the same thing that keeps them from learning nay other
>>     language, natural or artificial.  That is vocabulary.  Adults need a
>>     couple thousand words of vocabulary in order to comfortably talk
>>     about the things that they want to talk about, or maybe a lot more,
>>     and learning a couple of thousand words is HARD for most people.  No
>>     simplification of grammar is going to eliminate that problem.
>>
>>     lojbab
>>
>>
>> That is precisely my difficulties with Lojban. The grammar is easy as =
=F0,
>> but I'm still learning the actual vocab. I'll admit to being somewhat
>> lazy on the learning of it, but I'd say at this point I know ~1/4 of the
>> gismu and cmavo, a smattering of non-jvajvo lujvo, maybe a couple
>> fu'ivla, and a very few of the experimental words like zo'oi.
>>
>
> That was the point of the original LogFlash.  One of the few things that
> JCB did that was actually scientific was to test flashcard algorithms, an=
d
> the original LogFlash encoded what he found to be the optimal one. We
> eventually added some flexibility, but people turned away from the tested
> algorithm in favor of all sorts of flashcard approaches.
>
> But the original one really worked.  25 years on with little regular usag=
e
> for the last dozen years, and I still recall most of the gismu, having
> drilled them up to >95% in less than 2 months.  (I never completed the
> cmavo and rafsi to such a high degree, but the ones that I did learn I
> didn't forget very quickly.)
>
> Of course it is now a problem of converting the Turbo Pascal Dos program
> into something more portable.  (Eric Raymond tried to convert the program
> to portable C, but it never worked well.)


I've never been able to get that thing to work, actually. So right now I'm
using the Memrise.com lesson I made for the gismu, and another memrise one
someone else made for the cmavo.


